DevOps Internship - Week 1 Report

Topic : Linux Fundamentals, Linux Administration & Networking Basics

-> Introduction
This week I learned the basics of Linux and practiced using Ubuntu. I explored the Linux terminal,
worked with files and directories, learned how users and groups are managed, and understood why
Linux is important in DevOps.

-> Linux Fundamentals
Linux is an open-source operating system known for stability, security and flexibility. It is widely
used for servers, cloud platforms and DevOps automation.

-> Linux File System
Linux stores everything in a hierarchical structure starting from the root directory (/). Important
directories include /home for user files, /etc for configuration, /var for logs and /usr for installed
programs.

-> Users and Groups
Linux supports multiple users. Groups help manage permissions for several users at once. I
learned to create users, groups and assign users to groups.

-> File Permissions
Each file has read, write and execute permissions for the owner, group and others. Commands like
chmod and chown are used to manage access.

-> Package Management
Ubuntu uses the APT package manager. I practiced updating packages and installing software
using sudo apt update, sudo apt upgrade and sudo apt install.

-> Environment Variables
Environment variables store system settings. I learned to view them with printenv and create
temporary variables using export.

-> Basic Networking
I practiced hostname, hostnamectl, ip addr, ping and ifconfig to view system information and test
connectivity.

-> SSH
SSH is used for secure remote access to Linux systems. It allows administrators to manage servers
safely over a network.

-> 20 Common Linux Commands
pwd, ls, cd, mkdir, rmdir, touch, cp, mv, rm, cat, nano, grep, find, chmod, chown, ps, top, df -h, du
-sh, uname -a.

-> Conclusion
This week's activities improved my confidence in using Linux. I understand the basic commands,
file system, permissions, users, networking and package management, which will help me in
upcoming DevOps topics such as Docker, Jenkins and Kubernete